{{also|bulle|bullé}}
==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbʊlə/|lang=de}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etym|nds-de|de}}. More at [[bull]].

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m|Bullen|Bullen}}

# [[bull]] (male cattle).
# {{context|figurative|lang=de}} [[bull]] {{i|''a strong or stout man''}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|de|bullig}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|de|Stier}}

===Etymology 2===
Documented since the 19th century. Probably from {{term|Polizist|lang=de}} (or an abbreviation thereof), whose first syllable may in some dialects have become homophonous to [[#Etymology_1|Bulle]]. — Another possibility is a derivation from {{term|Puller|lang=de}} or {{term|Landpuller|lang=de}}, a non-derogatory term for a policeman of the 18th century.

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m|Bullen|Bullen}}

# {{context|mildly|pejorative|lang=de}} [[police officer]], [[cop]], [[pig]]

=====Usage notes=====
Although the word is only mildly pejorative in colloquial speech, it is punishable in Germany to use it towards or in reference to a police officer on duty.

=====Related terms=====
* [[Bullette]] {{i|female form; humorous}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|la|de}} ''[[bulla]]'' "seal capsule", originally meaning "ball, bullet".

====Noun====
{{de-noun|f||Bullen}}

# [[bull|Bull]] (papal decree).

